{"conjecture": "The reliability of extracted code from constructive proofs scales with the density of computational content in the proof's intermediate lemmas, not the length or elegance of the final proof term. Specifically, proofs that construct witnesses via bounded enumeration at each lemma boundary—rather than deferring construction to a single global step—yield extracted programs whose executable commitments are locally verifiable, reducing semantic gaps by an order of magnitude compared to direct extraction from monolithic proofs. This is falsifiable: given two extraction methods over the same specification, the lemma-bounded-enumeration method should produce programs with measurably fewer refinement-failures under property-based testing, and this advantage should correlate with the count of intermediate witness-producing lemmas, not total proof size."}
